---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import FaqAccordion from '../components/FaqAccordion.astro';
import { brand, domain } from '../data/cities';

const faqs = [
  { q: "Is a double closing legal in Texas?", a: "Double closings are a common structure for investor transactions across Texas. Each side is a separate, fully documented purchase and sale that runs through a title company. Legal questions about your specific contract belong with the settlement professional on your file." },
  { q: "Do both closings have to happen on the same day?", a: "They are typically scheduled back to back on the same day, and transactional funding is priced for that short hold. If the resale side moves to another day, the additional day fee in the published schedule applies for each extra day the funds are out." },
  { q: "Who chooses the title company?", a: "Often the title company your end buyer already uses can run both transactions. What matters most is an office that handles investor double closings regularly and is comfortable coordinating the two files in sequence." },
  { q: "What does the funding fee cover?", a: "The fee covers the use of the funds for your purchase side, and it follows the published schedule based on the funding amount. The full schedule and worked examples are on the transactional funding fees page." },
  { q: "Can I assign the contract instead of double closing?", a: "Sometimes. Assignment works when the contract allows it and everyone is comfortable seeing the fee. The double close vs assignment comparison walks through when each structure tends to fit." }
];

const jsonld = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "WebPage",
    "name": "How Double Closing Works in Texas",
    "url": `https://${domain}/how-double-closing-works-in-texas/`,
    "description": "The A-B and B-C sequence of a double closing in Texas, the paperwork each side needs, and where transactional funding fits.",
    "isPartOf": { "@type": "WebSite", "name": brand, "url": `https://${domain}/` }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": faqs.map((f) => ({ "@type": "Question", "name": f.q, "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a } }))
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": `https://${domain}/` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "How Double Closing Works in Texas", "item": `https://${domain}/how-double-closing-works-in-texas/` }
    ]
  }
];
---
<Base title="How Double Closing Works in Texas | San Antonio Wholesale Double Close" description="The A-B and B-C sequence of a double closing in Texas, the paperwork each side needs, and where transactional funding fits." path="/how-double-closing-works-in-texas/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <Header base="/" />
  <main id="main">
    <section class="guide-hero"><div class="container"><h1 class="guide-hero__title">How Double Closing Works in Texas</h1></div></section>

    <section class="section"><div class="container guide-prose">
      <p>A double closing is two separate real estate transactions on the same property, often on the same day. In the first transaction you buy the property from the seller. In the second you resell it to your end buyer. The closing team must confirm the funding, timing and repayment arrangements for the actual transaction.</p>
      <p>Because you take title in between, your purchase price and your resale price stay on separate closing statements. Your wholesale spread is the difference between the two transactions, not a fee line on a single assignment contract.</p>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section section--tint"><div class="container guide-prose">
      <h2 class="section__title">The A-B and B-C transactions</h2>
      <p>The first transaction is called A-B: the seller (A) sells to you (B) under your purchase contract. The second is B-C: you (B) sell to your end buyer (C) under a separate contract. Each transaction gets its own settlement statement, its own signatures and its own disbursement.</p>
      <p>The closing office coordinates the purchase and resale in the agreed sequence. Transactional funding may cover the purchase side, but the documents, buyer funds and repayment arrangements need review before either closing is completed.</p>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section"><div class="container guide-prose">
      <h2 class="section__title">How closings run in Texas</h2>
      <p>Texas closings typically run through a title company and its escrow officer rather than a closing attorney. Most residential resales use the promulgated contract forms published by the Texas Real Estate Commission, and the title company coordinates signatures, funding and recording with the county. One office can usually handle both transactions back to back, and the office your end buyer already uses is often the simplest choice.</p>
      <p>Texas does not charge a state transfer tax on real estate sales, which keeps the tax line items lighter than in many states. Each side of a double closing still carries its own closing costs, including title insurance, escrow fees and county recording fees. Title insurance premium rates are set at the state level, so the premium itself does not change between title companies, though other fees vary by office. Confirm the exact sequence and costs with the closing team on your deal, because each office has its own preferences for how the two files are scheduled.</p>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section section--tint"><div class="container guide-prose">
      <h2 class="section__title">The paperwork a file usually needs</h2>
      <p>The title company prepares and confirms exactly what your file requires. Across most Texas double closings, the paperwork includes:</p>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>A signed purchase and sale agreement for each side of the deal</li>
        <li>Title work on the property, ordered by the title company</li>
        <li>A settlement statement for each transaction showing its own price and costs</li>
        <li>Wire instructions and any payoff figures tied to the property</li>
        <li>Entity documents if you are buying through an LLC</li>
        <li>Association, estate or probate documents when the property calls for them</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Files with complete paperwork move fastest. Sending everything you have when you submit the deal keeps questions from surfacing at the closing table.</p>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section"><div class="container guide-prose">
      <h2 class="section__title">Where transactional funding fits</h2>
      <p>Our transactional funding is considered for your purchase side. The closing office confirms disbursement, resale and repayment arrangements before closing. The fee follows the published schedule on the <a href="/transactional-funding-fees/">transactional funding fees</a> page.</p>
      <p>Funding can be ready in as little as 24 hours once we have the deal details and the title company information. If you are still deciding between structures, the <a href="/double-close-vs-assignment/">double close vs assignment</a> guide compares the two honestly.</p>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section section--tint" id="faq"><div class="container faq__inner"><h2 class="section__title">Common questions about the process</h2><FaqAccordion items={faqs} /></div></section>

    <section class="final-cta"><div class="final-cta__graphic" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="container final-cta__inner"><h2 class="final-cta__title">Have a deal ready for funding?</h2><p class="final-cta__text">Submit the deal and we will review the numbers. Funding can be ready in as little as 24 hours once we have the deal details and title company information.</p><a class="btn btn--accent btn--lg" href="/#deal-form">Submit Your Deal <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
